--TEST--
`mixed as Core\Class` is `rule:types/unions-and-mixed`'s checked way out of `mixed` for a `Core` class too: it yields the instance or throws
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The conversion is a test and a throw, not an assertion: the value's runtime
// class is compared against the descriptor `Core\Time\Date` publishes, exactly
// as `is` compares it (`rule:types/conversion`).
mixed $date = Core\Time\Date::at(2026, 9, 17);
Core\Time\Date $back = $date as Core\Time\Date;
echo $back->format("yyyy-MM-dd"), "|\n";

// The result is the class, so its members are reachable with no second test
// and no nullable to unwrap.
mixed $uri = Core\Uri::parse("https://example.test/a?b=c");
echo ($uri as Core\Uri)->host(), "|\n";

// A value of another type throws where it is written rather than reaching the
// binding — including another `Core` class's instance, because a `Core` class
// answers for itself and has no hierarchy under it to walk.
try {
    Core\Time\Date $wrong = $uri as Core\Time\Date;
    echo "converted|\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "a uri is no date|\n";
}
mixed $seven = 7;
try {
    Core\Time\Date $scalar = $seven as Core\Time\Date;
    echo "converted|\n";
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "an int is no date|\n";
}

// `as ?Class` is no more a conversion here than it is for a declared class:
// `E0473` refuses the nullable spelling over any class target, and
// `is` is what asks the question without throwing.
--EXPECT--
2026-09-17|
example.test|
a uri is no date|
an int is no date|
